```mdl
feat F-18: Spec questions
  uses: [Spec, SpecArticle]
  desc: |
    A `question` in a spec's changelog is a choice nobody has made yet. It is answered in
    Composer, and the answer is recorded as a decision.

  req F-18.1: The Ledger lists the spec's questions as one of its filters, open ones first, and At cursor
              shows the open questions on the block at the editor's cursor.
  req F-18.2: Each question is drawn with the question component the chat uses: its context,
              its options, and a free-form answer field.
  req F-18.3: In an editable spec, an answer appends to `## Changelog` a decision by the person
              who answered, with `answers:` naming the question. A read-only spec lists its
              questions without the answer controls.
  req F-18.4: A question's `affects:` puts a chip on each block it names, and the chip opens the
              Ledger's questions.
```

A spec's document is the spec, and its text is `Spec.content`. It can be prose alone and take structure a piece at a
time: a line becomes a `feat` block, and the canvas and the board are drawn from the spec's `feat` blocks. In a
repository the same spec is a `.mdl` file with the same text.

```mdl
feat F-19: A spec's document is the spec
  req F-19.1: The spec's `feat` blocks are its features, in the order its text states them. No ECHO object holds a
             feature: the text is the only store.
  req F-19.2: A `feat` block states the feature's name (its title), status (`status:`, planned when it has none),
             description (`desc:`, or the block's prose), requirements (`req`), the features it depends on (`depends:`)
             and the spec that breaks it down (`spec:`).
  req F-19.3: No structure is required. A text may be prose alone, `feat` blocks may sit anywhere in it, and the prose
             around them is kept as written.
  req F-19.4: A feature added with `/feat` or an operation is a new `feat` block after the spec's last one, or at
             the end of the text when it has none.
  req F-19.5: A feature's block key names it to its task links, its showcases and its citations
             (`echo:///<spec>#<key>`). A block added in the app takes a fresh key, and keys are kept once given. Its
             `F-n` label is positional.
  req F-19.6: An edit from a view changes only the fields it names, through plugin-projects' `SetSpecBlock` and
             `DeleteSpecBlock` (org.dxos.plugin.projects#F-4.15). An empty field is removed from the block.
```

```mdl
feat F-20: Feature blocks in the editor
