```mdl
decision D-65: A section's fold toggle stays live while the rest of the section is inert, so one click folds or unfolds a section that does not have attention. Amends [F-20.7].
  date: 2026-11-04
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-20.7]
  why: Folding took two clicks, one to give the section attention and one on the toggle.
```

```mdl
decision D-66: Arrowing through a spec stops on each section instead of skipping it. A section the arrows reach has focus itself, so it is selected: it has attention and a focus ring. Enter goes into it, Escape in a field comes back out to the selection, and the arrows go on to the text on either side. Alt with Up or Down and Backspace act on the selected section as they would on a line. The stop belongs to the editor's block widgets, so object embeds in a Markdown document stop the same way. Amends [F-20.7] and [D-65].
  date: 2026-11-04
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-20.7]
  why: A section could be reached only with the mouse; the cursor skipped it.
```

```mdl
decision D-67: Replaces the part of [D-66] where the arrows go only to the text. Undo and redo on a selected section act on the document, and the section stays selected while it is still there. From a selected section, an arrow selects the next section on its side or puts the cursor in the text there, and does nothing when there is neither; Escape puts the cursor in the text after it, or before it when none follows; Backspace or Delete removes it and puts the cursor in the text before it, or after it when none precedes. The arrows pass a block that cannot be selected, such as a missing feature's notice. In a read-only document a selected section neither moves nor deletes.
  date: 2026-11-04
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-20.7]
  supersedes: D-66
  why: D-66 left open what undo, Escape, a delete and a move do with the selection and the cursor, and an arrow beside another section stops on it as an arrow from the text does.
```

```mdl
decision D-68: A feature embed in a spec is the editor's object embed, plugin-markdown's, the one a Markdown document draws; inside it is the feature's `section` surface. The section reads its attention from the embed. Like any embed it is inert until selected, its fold toggle too, so folding a section that is not selected takes a click to select it first; it shows its label and open button on hover and resizes from its bottom edge; a missing feature is its source text marked not found. plugin-markdown contributes the embed as `MarkdownCapabilities.ObjectEmbeds`, which plugin-projects' spec editor draws for every spec ([org.dxos.plugin.projects#D-198]), and the software package keeps only the sync, the pasted copy and `/feature`. CodePlugin depends on plugin-markdown, since without the embed a section is only its source text. Amends [F-20.7] and [D-65].
  date: 2026-11-04
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-20.7]
  supersedes: D-65
  why: The spec drew its sections in a frame of its own, a copy of the object embed; one embed keeps both documents the same.
```

```mdl
decision D-69: A spec's reviews are structured data. A SpecReview type sits beside the Spec type, and each review holds its verdicts keyed by the spec items they judge. The feature component draws the verdicts on requirements and a CodeMirror extension draws them on other items. An operation creates a review, with no UI of its own, as a tool for agents over MCP and in the app. GitHub file sync imports reviews into structured data. Reviews have a fixed format and are not extensible. A view of a whole review sits beside the spec, in its companion. An item's current status is its newest verdict, and old verdicts are kept.
  date: 2026-11-05
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-16, F-24]
```

```mdl
decision D-70: Each review is a separate ECHO object. Reviews are only imported from git, never written back for now, and only the most recent review is imported, with no backfill.
  date: 2026-11-05
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-16.1]
```

```mdl
decision D-71: Feature cards stay small and show no requirement verdicts; a feature's requirements and their verdicts are seen by going to the feature.
  date: 2026-11-05
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-24]
```

```mdl
decision D-72: A spec's reviews are ECHO objects beside the spec, one `SpecReview` per review, so a spec in a space keeps its review history. Each item's current status is its newest verdict in any review. Reviews come from git only: the `.review.mdl` file in the checkout or repository is imported and nothing writes one back, and only the current file is imported, with no backfill of earlier commits. The same front matter updates a review in place; new front matter makes a new review. The one way in for an agent is op:createSpecReview, which the Spec Review skill declares and the MCP server exposes. Requirement verdicts show where a feature's requirements are listed, its page and its section, and never on a feature card, which stays small. Fills in [D-69], [D-70] and [D-71], and amends [F-16.1] for a spec in a space, whose review is read from ECHO instead of the checkout.
  date: 2026-11-05
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-16.1, F-24]
  why: A review read from the checkout is lost to anyone without that checkout, and the file holds only the latest review.
  rejected:
    - Writing reviews back to git.
    - A review per item rather than per review.
    - Backfilling reviews from git history.
```

